{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe sibling admission runner fills fixture coverage once, at a single point in time. Two\nthings then reopen gaps: a criterion added to the registry later starts life with no\nfixture, and a criterion whose candidates all failed to reproduce is left with none. Without\na mechanism that revisits those gaps, coverage decays from its first day and the only repair\nis someone noticing.\n\nA repair loop that spends money unattended is itself a hazard, so the loop needs a bound\nthat does not depend on anyone watching it.\n\n## Approach\n\nA gap-only repair loop that converges and then costs nothing.\n\n**Gap-only scope.** Each run considers only criteria that\n`criteria.effective_routing('.', gate_key='plan_review')` reports and for which no spec file\nexists under `.rebar/evals/` or `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/`. Criteria that already have a\nfixture are never revisited, so the loop's work shrinks monotonically as coverage completes\nand reaches zero when every criterion is covered. This is what makes unattended spend\nbounded by construction rather than by a watchdog.\n\n**Circuit breaker.** The loop records, per criterion, how many consecutive runs failed to\nyield an admitted fixture. A run counts as a failure for that criterion in either of two\nways: the miner emitted candidates that then failed to reproduce, or the miner emitted\nnothing because the criterion was `skipped-unbalanced` (it could not produce a case on both\nsides of the fire/no-fire axis). On the third such run it creates a ticket titled\nexactly `unreliable-criterion: <criterion-id>` — the literal prefix `unreliable-criterion: `\nfollowed by the registry criterion id, with no other text in the title — and stops\nattempting that criterion. The sibling miner already skips a criterion carrying an open\nticket with that title, so the pair forms the breaker: while the ticket is open the criterion\ncosts nothing, and closing it after a rubric fix lets the criterion re-enter mining on the\nnext run.\n\n**Portability.** Recurring work must not depend on a CI provider. The trigger is\noperation-linked with an in-process due-stamp: the loop records its last run in the\nrepository's rebar state, and a rebar plan-review operation in the repository advances it\nwhen the configured interval has elapsed, defaulting to 30 days. It is also directly\ninvocable as a command, so an operator, a cron, or any CI provider can drive it, and a\nproject with no CI provider still converges through ordinary gate usage alone. No step\ndepends on a POSIX-only shell or on a `.rebar/` path resolved from the process working\ndirectory.\n\n**Unattended spend bound.** Mining is free, so only admission costs. Each run pre-flights\nthrough `ledger.reserve(..., cap_usd=25.0)` against the loop's own ledger file and stops when\nthe run's remaining headroom cannot cover the next criterion, leaving the rest for the next\ninterval. That bounds unattended spend to $25 per interval regardless of how many gaps exist.\n\n**Failure disposition.** A transient model or transport error inside a criterion's admission\nattempt is RETRYABLE and retried within the run. `BudgetExceeded` is FATAL for the run and\nnon-retryable: the run stops with completed criteria admitted and their spend reconciled, and\nthe next interval resumes. A criterion whose candidates fail to reproduce is not an error at\nall — it increments the breaker counter and the run continues to the next criterion. When a\ntransient failure and the ceiling coincide, the ceiling wins and the run stops.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe loop command, its due-stamp trigger and interval configuration, the per-criterion\nconsecutive-failure counter, the `unreliable-criterion:` ticket creation, and the loop's own\nledger file. It reuses the sibling miner and admission runner rather than duplicating either.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Repairing a criterion recorded as unreliable; that is the work the filed ticket represents.\n- Revisiting criteria that already have a fixture.\n- Removing or rewriting an existing fixture.\n- Any human curation step between mining and admission.\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nAdditive: a new command, a new state stamp, and a new ledger file. No existing command,\nspec, or persisted format changes. Back-out is deleting the command and its stamp; the\nstamp is the only new persisted state and nothing reads it if the command is absent, so\nremoval leaves the repository in its prior state with no data loss.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests drive the breaker counter, the gap-only selection, the due-stamp interval\narithmetic and the ledger stop condition with a stubbed admission runner, so each assertion\nis deterministic without a model. A subprocess test covers the command with no CI\nenvironment present. Ticket creation is exercised against a temporary store.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A criterion that already has a spec file is absent from a run's attempt list; verified\n      by `pytest` asserting the selected id set excludes it.\n- [ ] The third consecutive failed run creates exactly one ticket whose title equals\n      `unreliable-criterion: <criterion-id>`, and the first and second do not; verified by\n      `pytest` against a temporary store asserting the title string and the ticket count\n      after each run.\n- [ ] A run in which the miner reports the criterion `skipped-unbalanced` increments the same\n      counter as a reproduction failure; verified by `pytest` asserting the ticket is created\n      after three runs of mixed failure kinds.\n- [ ] A criterion with an open ticket of that title is absent from the attempt list; verified\n      by `pytest` asserting the id is not selected.\n- [ ] A run stops before attempting a criterion whose estimate exceeds the run's remaining\n      headroom, and admits the criteria it completed; verified by `pytest` against the real\n      `ledger.reserve` with `cap_usd=25.0`, asserting the admitted set and that the ledger\n      total is at most 25.0.\n- [ ] The command runs with no CI environment variables set and reports its attempt list;\n      verified by a `pytest` subprocess test with a cleared environment.\n- [ ] The due-stamp advances only after the configured interval has elapsed; verified by\n      `pytest` asserting no attempt occurs at interval minus one day and one occurs at the\n      interval.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n`make lint`, `make typecheck` and `make test` pass; a run over a store with a seeded gap\nattempts exactly that gap; a seeded three-failure history produces the ticket and the skip.\n\n## Budget\n\n**Hard cap $100** for building this child and its first runs. Each unattended run is\nseparately capped at $25 by its own pre-flight, and the loop's total cost falls to zero once\nevery criterion is covered or recorded as unreliable."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788320617419806534,"uuid":"a85da336-11eb-4d5a-8eb3-a23c464938bb"}